'''''Sedum gypsicola''''' is a [[flowering plant]] of the genus ''[[Sedum]]'' in the family [[Crassulaceae]]. 

==Taxonomy== 
''Sedum gypsicola'' is related to ''Sedum album'' and has been considered a synonym by some authors,<ref>{{cite web |title=Sedum gypsicola Boissier & Reuter, 1842 |url=https://www.crassulaceae.ch/de/artikel?akID=182&aaID=2&aiID=G&aID=184 |website=International Crassulaceae Network |access-date=18 October 2024}}</ref> but it differs in having papillate leaves and a more restricted distribution.<ref name="thart"></ref> Apart to the more widely distributed nominate [[subspecies]] ('''subsp. ''gypsicola'''''), Sicilian plants have been described as an [[endemic]] [[subspecies]], '''subsp. ''trinacriae'''''.<ref name="powo2">{{cite web |title=''Sedum gypsicola'' subsp. ''trinacriae'' Afferni |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:60468303-2 |website=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew |access-date=18 October 2024}}</ref>

==Distribution and habitat==
''Sedum gypsicola'' grows on [[gypsum]] and [[limestone]] at altitudes of 0 to 2000 m.<ref name="thart">{{cite book |last1='t Hart |first1=H. |editor1-last=Eggli |editor1-first=U. |title=Sedums of Europe: Stonecrops and Wallpeppers |date=2003 |publisher=A.A. Balkema Publishers |location=Lisse/Abingdon/Exton (PA)/Tokyo |page=69 |chapter=''Sedum gypsicola'' Boissier & Reuter 1842}}</ref> The species occurs in the [[Iberian Peninsula]] ([[Portugal]] and [[Spain]]), [[North Africa]] ([[Morocco]], [[Algeria]] and [[Tunisia]]) and [[Sicily]].<ref name="powo">{{cite web |title=''Sedum gypsicola'' Boiss. & Reut. |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:275486-1 |website=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew |access-date=18 October 2024}}</ref>

